    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d290">

                <pb facs="0527" n="512" sameAs="frus:frus1949v04/pg512"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">860S.00/7–1349: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Embassy in Italy </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">restricted</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-07-13T18:00:00-04:00">July 13, 1949—6 p.
                            m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1499. For urinfo, at his press conference this morning Secy was asked whether, in
                    view Tito statement<anchor n="1" xml:id="d290-f1"/> Yugo wld never agree return
                    of FTT Italy, US still favored return. Secy replied in affirmative; that US
                    position remained as stated Mar 20 proposal.</p>
                <p>Sent Rome 1499; rpt Trieste 301, Belgrade 368, Paris 2544, London 2430, USUN
                    361.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d290-f1" n="1" xml:id="d290-f1-content">
                <p>On July 10 Marshal Tito delivered a major speech at Pula (Pola), Slovenia in
                    which he reviewed all of Yugoslavia’s major foreign policy problems including
                    Carinthia, Trieste, Greece, relations with the USSR and the satellites, and
                    relations with the West. For extracts from that speech see Carlyle, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Documents on International Affairs, 1948–1949</hi>, pp.
                    459–460.</p>
                <p>In telegram 663, July 13, from Belgrade, Ambassador Cannon offered some critical
                    comment on Tito’s speech, and stated in regard to Trieste:</p>
                <p>“There was no hollowness here in contrast Carinthian question. Insistence on
                    Italian and Yugoslav views being given consideration and no unilateral action
                    taken reflects Yugoslav anxiety lest Four-Power agreement again sacrifices
                    Yugoslav interest in FTT. Also implicit is idea that problem susceptible of
                    solution by Yugoslav-Italo accord.” (760H.00/7–1349)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
